# FreeSail: the captain's orders for the merchant passage, Falmouth for Brest (spec M5
# §27, §29; package 36). The schooner's own book: the starter book is a choice and is
# not loaded by the scenario; `read the standing orders from
# data/standing_orders/starter.orders` adds its routines at any time.

# The cargo and the tide. With the anchor down in Carrick Road (the best bower let go at
# the start) forty tons of tin are bought at the quay by the list the supercargo brought
# off; the tin comes off by the port's lighter while the boat is ashore and is struck
# down when the boat is alongside again; the ebb serves from five (high water at
# Falmouth 04:50 by the world's tide; the pilot's word: "a ship takes the Road on the
# flood and leaves on the ebb"), and she gets under way on it, cast on the starboard
# tack with the head paying off to the eastward and steering S by E for the eastern
# channel (White 1835, p. 27: "the eastern channel is the better"). The conditions keep
# the rules to Falmouth: the anchor is let go and the boat comes alongside at Brest too.
standing order "the cargo": at the anchor let go, if the distance to Falmouth is under 5 miles then buy forty tons of tin
standing order "sail on the ebb": at the boat alongside, if the distance to Falmouth is under 5 miles then get under way on the starboard tack and steer S by E

# The pilot, outward and inward: the Falmouth pilot comes off to her as she stands out
# and leaves her beyond the outer road, the pilot of Brest comes off as she closes the
# road of Bertheaume; at each cutter's hail the fore topsail comes in so that she is slow
# enough for the boat ("shorten sail and he will come aboard"), and when he has boarded
# plain sail is made again. When the Falmouth pilot asks to be put off, his cutter coming
# off for him, she heaves to for the boat (a schooner with her sheets tended outran it,
# and the pilot was carried to the Iroise), fills away when he is in it, and makes plain
# sail and shapes her course again.
standing order "the pilot boards": at the pilot's hail then take in the fore topsail
standing order "the pilot aboard": at the pilot aboard, if the distance to Falmouth is under 9 miles then set plain sail
standing order "the pilot put off": at the pilot asks to be put off then heave to
standing order "the pilot off": at the pilot off then fill away
standing order "under way again": at filled away, if the distance to Falmouth is under 15 miles then set plain sail; shape a course for 50 00 N 4 57 W

# Out of the bay clear of the Manacles (the open sea's marks are given a berth of two or
# three miles, since they are reached by account and the account is a mile or two out
# by the afternoon; the Goulet's are a cable or two, with the land in sight and the
# bearings every five minutes): from the outer road a course a little east of
# south to a point off the Manacles' meridian (the ledge lies three miles and a half to
# the westward of the point, since she comes to it from wherever the pilot's boat left
# her, hove to and set down a mile), then south-south-west for a point six miles south-east of the
# Lizard (south-west would lie four points off a westerly wind, which she will not lay),
# and from there for the soundings seven leagues south-west of Ushant, which keeps her
# ten miles to the westward of the island and clear of the Fromveur.
standing order "clear of the road": when the distance to Falmouth outer road is under 0.6 miles then shape a course for 50 00 N 4 57 W
standing order "round the Manacles": when the distance to 50 00 N 4 57 W is under 2 miles then shape a course for 49 52 N 5 06 W
standing order "across the Channel": when the distance to 49 52 N 5 06 W is under 3 miles then shape a course for the soundings seven leagues south-west of Ushant

# The yards and the sheets: trimmed when she is steady on a course the book has shaped
# (the starter book's trim answers a shift of the true wind, not a turn of the ship),
# on a shift of a point, and the sheets tended every glass (the starter book's rule;
# Luce 1866 ch. XXIV).
standing order "trim to the course": at steady on the course then trim sails
standing order "trim on a shift": when the true wind veers 1 point or backs 1 point then trim sails
standing order "tend the sheets": every glass then trim the sheets

# The departure and the landfall: a bearing of the land every glass while it is in
# sight (N §1), so that the account is fixed on the Lizard before the land is lost and
# on Ushant when it is raised.
standing order "bearings": every glass, if the land is in sight then take a bearing of the land

# The course across is worked afresh from the account every hour, after the bearing of
# the land when there is one, since the leeway and the Channel's stream set her off a
# hundred-mile leg and the account is what he has to correct it by; the rule sleeps until
# Falmouth is eighteen miles astern (the Lizard's point rounded; the straight line from
# the bay crosses the Manacles, as the order's own warning says), when the soundings are
# within eight, and once the Passage de l'Iroise is within twenty, which it is only on
# the leg from the soundings (the soundings are a place seven leagues wide in Faden's
# words, reached within seven miles below).
standing order "the course across": every hour, if the distance to Falmouth is over 18 miles and the distance to the soundings seven leagues south-west of Ushant is over 8 miles and the distance to the Passage de l'Iroise is over 20 miles then shape a course for the soundings seven leagues south-west of Ushant

# The Iroise: from the soundings south-west of Ushant east for the Passage de l'Iroise,
# six leagues wide between the isles and the Raz, the common track to and from Brest
# (Faden 1793, p. 61); she is brought to there and the deep-sea lead hove, which reads
# the Iroise in her chart's words, forty to forty-five fathoms on putrid shells and
# mouldering rock (the note is read within a mile and a half of its mark, so she is
# brought to within that, the bearings every five minutes holding the account to it);
# then on by two points pricked on the chart, one south of the
# Gouemont and the Vandrée and one in the channel between La Parquette and the Basses
# du Lis (the Iroise's entrance, Faden p. 57), and so to the road of Bertheaume, where
# she anchors at the first cast under thirteen fathoms within a mile and a half of the
# road, standing in (her head west of north-east: the road shoals from twelve fathoms to
# the beach in a mile and a half, the account is not to be trusted to a cable there, and
# a mark reached by account is reached again whenever the account swings, which anchored
# her twice, once as she weighed on the flood steering east-south-east, whence the
# heading, and once as she cast with her head through north, whence the cast of the
# lead with three knots of way as the trigger); with the pilot aboard she anchors if she
# has not (the road's own mark is no trigger: she passes it on her way out to the
# Goulet). The
# course for the Iroise is worked afresh every hour on the way to it from the soundings
# (the leeway over twenty miles would carry her past the note's reach; with Ushant ten
# miles off and more, which it is on that leg and is not on the way down to the
# soundings, since from abreast of Ushant the line to the Iroise is laid through Molène
# and the Fromveur, which it was; and with the road of Bertheaume nine miles off and
# more, which it is on that leg and is not in the Rade, where the hour's order put her
# about for the Iroise once), and the bringing
# to is guarded so that it is done once: not again once she has filled away for the
# first point (the second point within seven miles, which it is from the Iroise's mark
# eastward and not before), since the account between the log's heaves runs on the mean
# heading and may put her back inside the mark for a glass; the deep-sea lead's cast is
# the only sounding there, so the filling away answers it alone (the hand lead goes in
# pilot water, fifteen miles on). Should she pass
# the Iroise's mark wide by account and cast nothing, the course for the first point is
# shaped once she is east of the Iroise all the same, since the straight course east
# from the Iroise ends on the Toulinguet.
standing order "the Iroise": when the distance to the soundings seven leagues south-west of Ushant is under 7 miles then shape a course for the Passage de l'Iroise
standing order "the course for the Iroise": every hour, if the distance to Ushant is over 10 miles and the distance to the Passage de l'Iroise is under 20 miles and the distance to the Passage de l'Iroise is over 2 miles and the distance to the road of Bertheaume is over 9 miles then shape a course for the Passage de l'Iroise
standing order "bring to in the Iroise": when the distance to the Passage de l'Iroise is under 1.5 miles and the distance to 48 18.3 N 4 43.5 W is over 7 miles then heave to
standing order "the bottom of the Iroise": at hove to, if the distance to the Passage de l'Iroise is under 5 miles then heave the deep-sea lead
standing order "fill away after the cast": at a sounding, if the distance to the Passage de l'Iroise is under 5 miles then fill away
standing order "for Bertheaume": at filled away, if the distance to the Passage de l'Iroise is under 5 miles then shape a course for 48 16.5 N 4 48 W
standing order "past the Iroise": when the distance to the Passage de l'Iroise is under 6 miles and the distance to 48 16.5 N 4 48 W is under 6 miles then shape a course for 48 16.5 N 4 48 W
standing order "mid-channel": when the distance to 48 16.5 N 4 48 W is under 1.5 miles then shape a course for 48 18.3 N 4 43.5 W
standing order "the road of Bertheaume": when the distance to 48 18.3 N 4 43.5 W is under 1.5 miles then shape a course for the road of Bertheaume
standing order "in the road": at a sounding, if the depth of water is under 13 fathoms and the distance to the road of Bertheaume is under 1.5 miles and the heading is west of NE and the speed is over 3 knots then come to an anchor
standing order "the pilot in the road": at the pilot aboard, if the distance to the road of Bertheaume is under 4 miles then come to an anchor

# The pilot comes off as she closes the road of Bertheaume (the fore topsail in at his
# hail, as above), and with him aboard she anchors in the road, since it is the ebb and
# no ship takes the Goulet against it (the pilot's words; the master's tide by his
# epitome says the same, high water at Brest in the afternoon), the anchoring the rule
# "in the road" above. At the turn to the flood, by daylight
# (the night's flood is let go by: the Goulet's marks are taken by bearings every five
# minutes, and the pilot aboard answers and does not con), she gets under way, cast on the
# larboard tack with her head to the eastward, and once under way the course is shaped
# from the account for the Goulet's mouth (she anchored a mile and a half short of the
# road's mark, whence no fixed heading from the anchor serves).
standing order "the flood": at the turn to the flood, if the daylight is day and the distance to the road of Bertheaume is under 4 miles then get under way on the larboard tack and steer E
standing order "for the Goulet": at under way, if the distance to the road of Bertheaume is under 4 miles then set plain sail; shape a course for 48 19.3 N 4 38 W

# The Goulet on the flood by the pass north of the Mingan and the Fillettes (Faden 1793,
# p. 57: range either shore and fear nothing, but not the middle), by points pricked on
# the chart in the deep water (twenty to forty-five metres by the chart's own grid, and
# four cables and more from the Mingan and the Fillettes as the chart's features place
# them, a mile and a half west of the grid's shoals) from the mouth, mid-channel between
# Petit Minou and the Toulinguet, under Petit Minou, and north of the Mingan and the
# Fillettes to the inner end under Portzic, and then
# for the Bay of Brest; in pilot water a bearing is taken every five minutes (from
# fourteen miles off the road of Bertheaume, which is the Iroise's mark, since the marks
# from there in are reached by account within a mile or two and the account between the
# log's heaves swings) and the hand lead kept going within six miles of the Goulet's
# middle, the road to the Bay (the Regulations of 1806, the Pilot's articles; the
# Master's art. XXIX).
standing order "the mouth of the Goulet": when the distance to 48 19.3 N 4 38 W is under 0.4 miles then shape a course for 48 20.1 N 4 36.5 W
standing order "under Petit Minou": when the distance to 48 20.1 N 4 36.5 W is under 0.4 miles then shape a course for 48 20.7 N 4 35 W
standing order "north of the Mingan": when the distance to 48 20.7 N 4 35 W is under 0.4 miles then shape a course for 48 20.7 N 4 34.3 W
standing order "north of the Fillettes": when the distance to 48 20.7 N 4 34.3 W is under 0.4 miles then shape a course for 48 21 N 4 33 W
standing order "under Portzic": when the distance to 48 21 N 4 33 W is under 0.4 miles then shape a course for 48 20.8 N 4 31.5 W
standing order "into the Bay": when the distance to 48 20.8 N 4 31.5 W is under 0.4 miles then shape a course for the Bay of Brest
standing order "pilot water": every 5 minutes, if the distance to the road of Bertheaume is under 14 miles and the land is in sight then take a bearing of the land
standing order "the lead in pilot water": every 5 minutes, if the distance to 48 20.8 N 4 33.9 W is under 6 miles then heave the lead

# The Bay of Brest: anchor where you please in eight, ten or sixteen fathoms on mud
# (Faden): at the first cast under twelve fathoms within the Bay (the flats inside Portzic
# rise from eleven fathoms to two in half a mile), or at the Bay's mark;
# the boat ashore for the prices, and the tin sold at the quay.
standing order "in the Bay": at a sounding, if the distance to the Bay of Brest is under 3 miles and the depth of water is under 12 fathoms and the speed is over 2 knots then come to an anchor
standing order "the mark in the Bay": when the distance to the Bay of Brest is under 0.5 miles then come to an anchor
standing order "the agent": at brought up, if the distance to Brest is under 6 miles then send the boat ashore with the mate
standing order "the tin sold": at the boat alongside, if the distance to Brest is under 6 miles then sell forty tons of tin
